# Diagnostic environment v4: the GPT U-Boot reads is valid, so what does the
# backup table say?
#
# v3 ruled out the 512-byte-sector theory outright: byte 512 of LUN0 (U-Boot's
# LBA 0, right after the protective MBR) is all zeros, so the only GPT on LUN0
# is the 4 KiB one at LBA 1. That header parses as:
#   current_lba 1, backup_lba 0xeaa7ff (== last LBA of the LUN),
#   first_usable 6, last_usable 0xeaa7fa, pte_lba 2,
#   num_partition_entries 0x20 (32), pte_size 0x80 -> the whole array is one
#   4 KiB block, of which entries 1..14 are used.
# So U-Boot is not misreading anything, and 32 slots really do contain just the
# fourteen partitions v2 printed. No boot, dtbo, vbmeta, modem, xbl.
#
# Meanwhile v3 found the small LUNs do carry tables of their own: LUN1 has two
# partitions and one of them is literally called xbl (entry 1, start 0x1,
# 0x380 LBAs = 3.5 MiB); LUN3 is 32 MiB with three partitions; LUN2 has an
# "EFI PART" signature at LBA 1 but a table U-Boot refuses to accept. Nineteen
# partitions in total, against the forty-odd a working RMX1971 needs.
#
# Which leaves one strong candidate: this primary table is not the factory one.
# A repartition that writes only LBA 1/2 leaves the backup table untouched at
# the end of the LUN, and find_valid_gpt() prefers the primary -- so a loader
# reading the primary sees 14 partitions while anything that ends up on the
# backup sees the original layout. Dumping the backup header and its first
# entry costs seven lines and settles it.
#
# Read-only, as always.
bootdelay=2
bootretry=-1

stdin=serial,button-kbd
stdout=serial,vidconsole
stderr=serial,vidconsole

preboot=echo; echo RMX1971 diag v4

# The five partitions on the small LUNs, by index: name,start,size.
diag_l1=setenv n;setenv s;setenv z;part name scsi 1 1 n;part start scsi 1 1 s;part size scsi 1 1 z;echo L1.1=$n,$s,$z;setenv n;setenv s;setenv z;part name scsi 1 2 n;part start scsi 1 2 s;part size scsi 1 2 z;echo L1.2=$n,$s,$z
diag_l3=setenv n;setenv s;setenv z;part name scsi 3 1 n;part start scsi 3 1 s;part size scsi 3 1 z;echo L3.1=$n,$s,$z;setenv n;setenv s;setenv z;part name scsi 3 2 n;part start scsi 3 2 s;part size scsi 3 2 z;echo L3.2=$n,$s,$z;setenv n;setenv s;setenv z;part name scsi 3 3 n;part start scsi 3 3 s;part size scsi 3 3 z;echo L3.3=$n,$s,$z

# LUN2: header fields plus the first type GUID, to see why validation fails.
diag_l2=scsi dev 2;scsi read 0xa0001000 1 1;md.b 0xa0001000 0x60;scsi read 0xa0002000 2 1;md.b 0xa0002000 16

# The backup GPT: header at the last LBA, and (assuming a 32-entry table) its
# first partition entry one LBA earlier -- first 16 bytes are the type GUID, the
# name starts at offset 0x38 as UTF-16LE, so md's ASCII column spells it out.
diag_bak=scsi dev 0;scsi read 0xa0000000 0xeaa7ff 1;md.b 0xa0000000 0x60;scsi read 0xa0000000 0xeaa7fe 1;md.b 0xa0000000 16;md.b 0xa0000038 16

# Baseline for the cache partition (primary table says start 0xcd00): if a
# marker flashed with `fastboot flash cache` shows up here, the bootloader and
# U-Boot agree on at least that part of the layout and we have a transfer path.
diag_cache=scsi dev 0;scsi read 0xa0000000 0xcd00 1;md.b 0xa0000000 32

bootcmd=echo === l1;run diag_l1;echo === l3;run diag_l3;echo === l2;run diag_l2;echo === backup gpt;run diag_bak;echo === cache;run diag_cache;echo === end;sleep 30;reset
